.TH UPWGEN 1 "October 2026" "upwgen 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
upwgen \- generate cryptographic passwords and Unicode authentication test data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B upwgen
.RI [ generate | corpus ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ length " [" count ]]
.br
.B upwgen
.BR sets " | " help " | " version
.SH DESCRIPTION
upwgen generates one value at a time using operating-system cryptographic
randomness. The default is one 24-code-point password from the portable ASCII
profile, written as raw UTF-8 followed by a newline.
Length counts Unicode code points, not UTF-8 bytes or displayed graphemes.
.PP
The portable profile includes ASCII letters, digits, and symbols.
The unicode profile uses assigned, visible, NFC-stable characters from the
Unicode 16.0.0 catalog.
The unspeakable profile includes tag characters, combining marks, emoji,
historic scripts, Braille, game symbols, and ASCII.
The stress profile is an alias for unspeakable.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --profile " NAME"
Select portable, unicode, or unspeakable.
.TP
.BI --length " N"
Request exactly N code points, from 1 to 4096. Default: 24.
.TP
.BI --count " N"
Generate N values, from 1 to 10000. Default: 1.
Length multiplied by count must not exceed 1000000.
.TP
.BI --max-bytes " N"
Limit each value to N UTF-8 bytes, from 1 to 16384.
Impossible or excessively restrictive requests fail.
.TP
.BI --include " LIST"
Replace the selected categories with a comma-separated list.
The portable profile permits ASCII categories only.
.TP
.BI --exclude " LIST"
Remove the listed categories.
.TP
.BI --require " LIST"
Require at least one member of every listed, selected category.
Selected categories are optional by default.
.TP
.BI --format " FORMAT"
Choose raw, numbered, escaped, or jsonl.
Raw output is one value per line with no metadata.
Numbered output is for interactive display.
Escaped output lists code points.
JSONL emits one JSON object per value, including encoding lengths and category
metadata.
.TP
.BI --seed " N"
Corpus only: use an unsigned 64-bit seed. Default: zero.
Seeded output is reproducible test data and must never be used for credentials.
The generate command rejects this option.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display usage.
.TP
.B --version
Display release and Unicode data versions.
.SH CATEGORIES
symbols, upper, lower, digits, tags, combining, emoji, historic, braille, games.
The sets command lists descriptions and profile counts.
The unicode profile has no permitted tag or combining category members.
.SH LEGACY OPTIONS
Exclusion flags can be combined:
-s symbols, -c upper, -l lower, -d digits, -n tags, -e emoji,
-x historic, -g games, -m combining, and -o braille.
Positional length and count remain accepted.
Legacy options do not change the profile or require selected categories.
.SH CORPUS
The corpus command defaults to the unspeakable profile, JSONL output, and seed
zero. It uses a portable SplitMix64 test generator. Its values are public test
data, not secrets.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
upwgen --length 32 --count 5
upwgen --include upper,lower,digits --require digits
upwgen --profile unicode --length 20 --max-bytes 64
upwgen --profile unspeakable --format escaped
upwgen corpus --seed 42 --count 100
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. Nonzero indicates invalid input, entropy failure,
allocation failure, output failure, or exhausted constrained-sampling limits.
Diagnostics go to standard error. A failed streaming batch may have already
emitted complete values; callers must check the exit status.
.SH SECURITY
Native generation uses getrandom on Linux, BCryptGenRandom on Windows, and
arc4random_buf on Apple and BSD systems.
Bounded random choices use rejection sampling.
Requirements and byte limits reject entire candidates, preserving uniformity
among accepted values. Sampling stops after 100000 candidates per value or
4000000 sampled code points across a batch.
.PP
Secret buffers are cleared as a best-effort measure. Terminal history,
redirected output, the operating system, and other applications can retain
copies. Unicode passwords require testing through the entire authentication
workflow; destinations may normalize, truncate, reject, or remove characters.
.SH AUTHOR
Jon Hood.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation: https://squinky86.github.io/upwgen/
